window.docContents[84].resolve({"/Tier-2___-definitions/Computational___Specifications___/Computational.Specifications.Assumptions.Kyber1024Correct/#file-Computational___Specifications___Assumptions___Kyber1024Correct":{"contents":"Paper › Computational/Specifications/Assumptions › Kyber1024Correct.leanTier 2 · definitions\n\nTier 2 · definitions used by the computational proof 2 headline theorems depend on it 54 of 101 lines trusted trust banner B2, trusted: definitions used by the statement source ↗Read the definitions shown. Only the declarations that the headline statements use are shown, with the namespace, section, open and variable commands needed to read them. The collapsed runs are theorems, which Lean checks, and definitions that no headline statement uses.\n\nCBD-Noise Idealization Hypothesis for Round-3 CRYSTALS-Kyber-1024 (KyberNoiseGap)Trusted: definitions used by the statement. Defines sampleNoise128, sampleNoiseVec, kyber1024RealKpkeFailsExp, kyber1024IdealKpkeFailsExp, and KyberNoiseGap for proving the concrete Kyber-1024 δ_kem ≤ ε + 1 / 2 ^ 112 bound (Kyber1024KemDeltaCorrect in Computational/Specifications/LibsignalCli/Kyber1024Delta.lean; see DEV-PQ21 in Computational/docs/DEVIATIONS.md and Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md).In deterministic Kyber-1024, the 8 + 9 CBD_2 noise blocks of key generation and encapsulation are pseudorandomly expanded by SHA3-512 (G) and SHAKE-256 (PRF) from the 64-byte seed and 32-byte encapsulation randomness, so no cryptographically small bound can be proved without a pseudorandomness or random-oracle assumption on SHA3-512 / SHAKE-256. KyberNoiseGap ε isolates that single pseudorandomness step on the fixed efficient test kyber1024KpkeFailsWith sampleNtt, leaving the SHAKE-128 matrix Â (sampleNtt), SHA3-256 message hash H(r), polynomial arithmetic, compression rounding, and Fujisaki–Okamoto re-encryption completely concrete.\n\n\n\ndef sampleNoise128L42–49 ↗#Sample 128 uniform bytes (4 × 32 bytes via sampleBytes32) as List (BitVec 8), matching the 128-byte input length of CryptoGames.MLKEM.samplePolyCbd2.\n\ndef sampleNoiseVecL51–55 ↗#Sample n independent 128-byte uniform CBD_2 input blocks, indexed as Fin n → List (BitVec 8).\n\ndef kyber1024RealKpkeFailsExpL57–69 ↗#Real-noise honest K-PKE decryption-failure experiment: draws uniform 64-byte seed and 32-byte r as in KemDeltaCorrect and evaluates kyber1024KpkeFailsWith sampleNtt on the concrete SHA3-512 / SHAKE-256 CBD_2 blocks kyber1024RealNoiseKG and kyber1024RealNoiseEnc.\n\ndef kyber1024IdealKpkeFailsExpL71–82 ↗#Ideal-noise honest K-PKE decryption-failure experiment: draws uniform 64-byte seed and 32-byte r together with independent uniform 128-byte CBD_2 blocks nKG (8 blocks) and nEnc (9 blocks), and evaluates the identical kyber1024KpkeFailsWith sampleNtt predicate.\n\ndef KyberNoiseGapL84–99 ↗#Pseudorandomness idealization gap of SHA3-512 (G) and SHAKE-256 (PRF) for the single fixed efficient test of honest Round-3 CRYSTALS-Kyber-1024 K-PKE decryption failure (DEV-PQ21): bounds the failure probability under real SHA3-512/SHAKE-256 CBD_2 noise by that under uniform CBD_2 noise plus ε. If SHAKE-256 is modeled as a random oracle, the real and ideal experiments coincide unless σ = coins, giving ε ≤ Pr[σ = coins] (= 2^-256 when SHA3-512 is also modeled as a random oracle, since its 32-byte input d and 64-byte input m ‖ H(pk) have distinct lengths; ε = 0 when the key-generation and encapsulation PRF calls are modeled as independent random functions, analogous to ε_ro in KeyIndRoGap, DEV-CLI16). Without random oracles, the key-generation noise blocks reduce to PRG security of SHA3-512 on uniform 32-byte d plus PRF security of SHAKE-256 keyed by σ, whereas on the encapsulation side m = SHA3-256(r) enters the decryption check alongside coins = SHA3-512(m ‖ H(pk))[32..64] (a public unkeyed hash of m and H(pk)), so KyberNoiseGap ε directly states that replacing those SHA3-512/SHAKE-256-derived blocks by uniform bytes changes the fixed kyber1024KpkeFailsWith sampleNtt failure probability by at most ε.\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"Computational/Specifications/Assumptions/Kyber1024Correct.lean","id":"/Tier-2___-definitions/Computational___Specifications___/Computational.Specifications.Assumptions.Kyber1024Correct/#file-Computational___Specifications___Assumptions___Kyber1024Correct"}});